From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] aic_load_fw: return an errno from a failed configuration or upload

Pinned tag plus the series up to
aic_load_fw-no-rx-urb-in-flight-after-the-bus-stop; kernel 6.18.53).

aicwf_usb_probe() reaches system_config() and aicfw_download_fw() with
ret still holding aicwf_bus_start()'s 0. Both failure branches jump to
out_free_bus without setting it, so the probe frees usb_dev, clears the
interface data, prints "failed with errno 0" at error level and returns
0. The driver core then counts the interface as bound (really_probe() in
drivers/base/dd.c treats 0 as success): no other probe runs, a manual
bind is refused as busy, and the radio stays down until the part is
re-plugged or the module reloaded, with no errno anywhere in the log.

In the vendor driver: the two reboot exits just above
(system_reboot(), rwnx_send_reboot()) return 0 through the same label on
purpose -- the part leaves the bus and re-enumerates, and a new probe
follows. The configuration and upload failures share the label but not
that property: the part stays on the bus at its loader id, and nothing
probes it again.

What changes:
- system_config()'s result goes into ret. It carries the errno of the
  firmware message that failed (-EPIPE, -ENOMEM from rwnx_send_msg()), or
  -1 for an unknown chip, which becomes -EIO. A timed-out message returns
  0: cmd_mgr_queue() returns 0 after the timeout, and cmd_complete()
  overwrites the -ETIMEDOUT it stored. The timeout marks the command
  manager crashed, so the next message returns -EPIPE; only a timeout on
  the last message goes unseen (see aic_load_fw-a-timed-out-firmware-message-fails-and-is-freed). Not changed here.
- A failed aicfw_download_fw() sets -EIO: every failure branch of the
  upload returns a bare -1 (= -EPERM), which names nothing.
- The stop-race stimulus of aic_load_fw-no-rx-urb-in-flight-after-the-
  bus-stop, which takes this exit to mirror a failed configuration, sets
  -EIO the same way, so it keeps mirroring it.

The reboot exits are left alone: returning an error there would print a
probe failure for what is the expected path of every cold boot.

Not taken:
- -ENODEV: the driver core logs it at debug level as "rejects match",
  which would hide a real failure.
- Propagating the upload's -1: -EPERM reads as a permission problem.

Falsifier, on the board, with the part at its loaded id:
  modprobe -r aic8800_fdrv aic_load_fw
  modprobe aic_load_fw rx_loader_inject_stop_race=1
  (replug or rebind the part so that it enumerates at its loader id)
Without the fix: "failed with errno 0", and the interface stays listed
under /sys/bus/usb/drivers/aic_load_fw/. With it: "failed with errno -5",
the driver core's "probe with driver aic_load_fw failed with error -5",
and the interface is not listed. One listing with the fix refutes it.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic_load_fw/aicwf_usb.c b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic_load_fw/aicwf_usb.c
index 3899334..7919775 100644
--- a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic_load_fw/aicwf_usb.c
+++ b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic_load_fw/aicwf_usb.c
@@ -1978,6 +1978,7 @@ static int aicwf_usb_probe(struct usb_interface *intf, const struct usb_device_i
         rx_loader_inject_stop_race--;
         stop_race_pending = true;
         usb_err("stop race stimulus: error path after the bus start\n");
+        ret = -EIO;
         goto out_free_bus;
     }
 
@@ -1996,11 +1997,21 @@ static int aicwf_usb_probe(struct usb_interface *intf, const struct usb_device_i
 		goto out_free_bus;
 	}
 
-    if (system_config(usb_dev)) {
+    /* A failed configuration or upload leaves the part on the bus at its
+     * loader id, and no re-enumeration follows, unlike the two reboot
+     * exits above. Return an errno, so the driver core does not keep the
+     * interface bound to a probe that has freed everything. The upload
+     * returns a bare -1 (= -EPERM), which names nothing; say -EIO.
+     */
+    ret = system_config(usb_dev);
+    if (ret) {
+        if (ret == -1)
+            ret = -EIO;
         goto out_free_bus;
     }
 
-    if (aicfw_download_fw(usb_dev)){
+    if (aicfw_download_fw(usb_dev)) {
+        ret = -EIO;
         goto out_free_bus;
     }
     
